/* Maui film-canon personality rebuild 11-20; source: Book/Maui folder. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const edits={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-11":{"number":11,"title":"The Tide Takes Inventory","content":"The morning after the festival, I found the welcome sign floating face down in the cove.\n\nAt first, I thought someone had played a joke.\n\nMaui had been very proud of that sign. It was carved from a piece of driftwood with a curve like a smile, painted in a blue that matched the thread holding my brass key. WELCOME TO TIDEGLASS COVE, it said. SAFE LANDING AT ALL TIDES, it said beneath.\n\nThe second line had been his idea.\n\nI waded in far enough to reach the sign. The water was cold. A small crab took offense to my ankle. I took offense to the crab.\n\nBy the time I dragged the sign onto the sand, Maui had appeared with a spear in one hand and the expression of a man who had expected to fight something large.\n\n\"Who's attacking?\"\n\n\"The ocean.\"\n\nHe looked at the sign.\n\n\"The ocean has terrible manners.\"\n\n\"The ocean also appears to have won.\"\n\nMaui planted the spear in the sand. His tattoos shifted as he crouched beside the wood.\n\n\"Probably a wave.\"\n\n\"Probably several.\" I pointed toward the dock.\n\nThe outermost post leaned at an angle that had not been there yesterday. Beyond it, the new landing boards sat crooked above the tide. One rope had worn almost through where it passed around the mooring ring.\n\nMaui's smile disappeared.\n\n\"Nobody's hurt,\" he said.\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nHe stood. \"I'll fix it.\"\n\nI caught the edge of his wrist before he could take three long heroic strides into the water.\n\n\"That's not the same as checking whether it's safe.\"\n\n\"I built it.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why I'm asking you to look.\"\n\nHe stared down at my hand. I let go. His pride had begun to rise, hot and quick, but so had his worry.\n\nHe looked at the dock again.\n\n\"All right,\" he said, more quietly. \"We look.\"\n\nWe fetched Fen Arlow, the canal steward, who arrived carrying a measuring stick and an expression that suggested he had already heard about our sign.\n\n\"Safe at all tides,\" he read.\n\n\"I would like to submit an amendment,\" I said.\n\nFen stepped onto the first plank, pressed it with the heel of his boot, and stepped back.\n\n\"Good instinct. The outer post is seated in sand, not stone. The channel shifts after the first hard autumn tide.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms. \"It held through the festival.\"\n\n\"And it was a spectacular festival,\" Maui added, jabbing a thumb toward his chest. \"You should have seen the welcome sign. Practically famous.\"\n\nMini Maui crawled across his shoulder and drew a wobbly line in the air to represent the post. Then the tiny figure toppled sideways and disappeared behind the curve of Maui's arm.\n\n\"Unhelpful,\" Maui muttered.\n\nFen tapped the tide mark. \"It was safe for the water we had. Not every water we'll get.\"\n\nMaui stared at the driftwood lettering he'd painted himself. He hated that the sign was wrong almost as much as he hated watching somebody else be right. He could probably carve another in an afternoon. That wouldn't make a dangerous landing safe.\n\n\"All right,\" he said, too loudly. \"New motto: MOSTLY SAFE WHEN FEN SAYS SO.\"\n\nFen raised one eyebrow.\n\n\"Fine. You write it. I make it look magnificent.\"\n\nI heard the disappointment underneath the joke. For him, the good intentions and the beautiful sign had been the same thing. They weren't. I squeezed his hand once and let Fen continue explaining the high-tide mark.\n\n\"Your festival happened at low tide.\"\n\n\"It was a very successful festival.\"\n\n\"Congratulations. The sea doesn't attend council meetings.\"\n\nI nearly smiled. Maui did not.\n\nFen showed us the dark line water had left against the rocks. It was well above the welcome sign's promised safe level.\n\n\"We need a new post,\" Fen said. \"Maybe two. And until then, that landing is closed.\"\n\nMaui picked up the sign. \"I can replace the whole thing before lunch.\"\n\n\"With what timber?\" Fen asked.\n\n\"The trees.\"\n\n\"Which trees?\"\n\n\"The—\" Maui gestured toward the hillside. \"Trees.\"\n\n\"Somebody owns those. Somebody else planted the young ones to stop the bank from sliding. You cannot solve one problem by creating another.\"\n\nMaui's face did something complicated.\n\nI recognized the moment. He was being told that strength was not enough, and he was struggling not to hear it as proof that he was not enough.\n\n\"I'll find the wood,\" he said.\n\nFen nodded. \"Good. Get permission. Bring it to the yard. We'll test it.\"\n\nThat afternoon the three of us walked the shoreline with Tamsin Vale, the lanternwright, who had once helped maintain the cove steps. Her tool bag clinked when she moved.\n\n\"How did your sign end up in the water?\" she asked.\n\n\"Poor leadership,\" I said.\n\nMaui shot me a look.\n\n\"Not just yours,\" I added. \"Mine too. I approved the wording.\"\n\nTamsin touched the scraped paint with a thumbnail. \"Then you can both approve the correction.\"\n\nWe spent an hour marking tide heights on the stone steps. Another hour counting what the cove actually had: four sound ropes, one damaged mooring ring, a stack of timber too narrow for the outer rail, two usable lantern brackets and a remarkably stubborn crab that refused to vacate the supply basket.\n\nBy sunset, my notebook held three pages of disappointing facts.\n\nMaui stood over my shoulder.\n\n\"I thought this place would be easier.\"\n\n\"Because it's magical?\"\n\n\"Because it wanted us here.\"\n\nI closed the notebook. \"A place can welcome us and still need maintenance.\"\n\nHe sat beside me on the step. The wind lifted his hair from his shoulders. Salt had dried along one edge of his tattooed arm.\n\n\"I wanted to build you somewhere you could come back to,\" he said.\n\n\"You did.\"\n\n\"It broke.\"\n\n\"The dock broke. The choice didn't.\"\n\nHe watched the dark water.\n\nI leaned my shoulder against his. For once, he did not make a speech about being impressive. He leaned back.\n\nFen had watched us scrape the false promise from the sign. They came down from the upper path with a small slate of tide times and asked where we'd put the new information.\n\n\"On a separate board?\" I suggested.\n\n\"Yes. Somewhere people can check it before they launch.\"\n\nMaui looked at the little crab he'd painted. \"But the sign is beautiful.\"\n\n\"It can be beautiful,\" Fen said. \"It can't be a substitute for a changing water chart.\"\n\nWe set a narrow notice beneath the shelter with room for the day's safe landing window, the name of whoever checked it, and a clear route to the upper path when the cove was closed. Lio tried reading it while carrying a crate. She made us move it closer to the place where a person actually needed to decide.\n\nMaui stood back, frowning. \"People are going to think I didn't know what I was doing.\"\n\n\"You didn't,\" Lio said cheerfully.\n\nHe stared at her. She stared back.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\nI wrote down that the weather could change after the morning check and that anyone at the landing should follow the posted closure, even if the water looked calm. We hadn't invented a way to make the sea predictable. We'd made one small choice easier for the people who used it.\n\nMaui touched the blue thread at my wrist with one finger. \"Will you go through the doors again?\"\n\n\"Yes. When I want to.\"\n\n\"I might feel lonely.\"\n\n\"You're allowed to. I'm ethically non-monogamous, and I prefer polyamory. Our choosing each other doesn't mean I won't choose to visit people elsewhere.\"\n\nHe took a breath. \"Can I ask when you'll be back without sounding like a guard?\"\n\n\"You can ask. I can tell you what I know. We don't have to pretend a guess is a promise.\"\n\nHe thought that over, then held out the paintbrush. \"Will you help me make a better crab?\"\n\n\"The crab is excellent.\"\n\n\"I want a second opinion.\"\n\nI leaned against him, looking at the tiny creature beneath the truthful sign. It had survived the tide and our argument. I was beginning to think that was more than enough for a little piece of driftwood.\n\n\"People keep asking you what the doors mean,\" he said. \"Who you'll visit. How often you'll come back.\"\n\n\"People can be curious. It doesn't mean they get to vote.\"\n\n\"I know.\" He hesitated. \"I'm trying to remember what you said. About loving people without locking them up.\"\n\nI touched the little brass key at my throat. \"I meant it.\"\n\n\"So did I. About wanting to try, even when the questions are stupid.\"\n\n\"Do you have one?\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"If somebody else builds you something that doesn't break, would you stop wanting to come here?\"\n\nIt hurt, because I could hear how carefully he had made the question small.\n\n\"Maui.\" I turned toward him. \"I want to come here because this is home. Not because the dock is perfect, and not because somebody won me at a festival.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"And if I love someone else someday, I'm still responsible for what I promise you. I don't get to leave you guessing. Neither do you.\"\n\nHe let that settle.\n\n\"All right.\"\n\n\"All right?\"\n\n\"I think I can live with a promise that involves actual work.\"\n\n\"Revolutionary.\"\n\nHe grinned, finally.\n\nThen he leaned down to kiss me, stopped close enough for his breath to warm my cheek, and waited.\n\n\"Yes,\" I whispered.\n\nHis lips were soft despite every boast he'd ever made about them. He smelled like salt and the wood shavings caught in his clothes. The first kiss of the evening became a second when I tugged lightly at his hand.\n\nSomewhere above us, the first lantern came on.\n\nWe stayed until it was too dark to count posts.\n\nThe next morning, before the bakery opened, we took the welcome sign to the workshop. Tamsin sanded away the damaged line while I painted new letters in careful white strokes.\n\nWELCOME TO TIDEGLASS COVE.\n\nCHECK THE TIDE CHART BEFORE LANDING.\n\nMaui regarded the result.\n\n\"Less dramatic.\"\n\n\"More accurate.\"\n\n\"I could add a sea monster.\"\n\n\"On the safety sign?\"\n\n\"Only a small one.\"\n\nI handed him the brush.\n\nHe painted a tiny crab at the bottom instead.\n\nI liked it better that way.\n\nFor the first time, the sign told the truth. And when we carried it back down to the shore, nobody had to pretend that made the water harmless.","word_count":1739},"maui-12":{"number":12,"title":"A Place for the Departing","content":"The first person to ask whether they were allowed to leave the Lanternweald without saying goodbye was standing in my kitchen.\n\nThey were holding a loaf of bread in both hands. Mara had baked it that morning, and the crust had split along the top like a small mountain range. The traveler had bought it with two copper buttons and an offer to mend the bakery curtains.\n\n\"I can give the bread back,\" they said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"If it makes things easier.\"\n\nI stared at them. They stared at the floor.\n\nIt was the third time they had come to see me that week, and the first time they'd been able to ask the question.\n\n\"You can leave,\" I said. \"You don't owe anyone a reason.\"\n\nThe traveler's shoulders loosened. Not all the way.\n\n\"What about the register?\"\n\n\"Neri's arrival book?\"\n\nThey nodded.\n\n\"It's not supposed to be a leash.\"\n\n\"Everyone knows when I come in. Pip reads out the visitor names sometimes. I don't want anyone knowing which road I'm taking.\"\n\nI had assumed that a place full of doors would make leaving simple. I'd assumed many things since arriving here. My notebook was becoming a monument to the hazards of assuming.\n\n\"Can you wait until I check how the departure book works?\" I asked.\n\nThey hesitated.\n\n\"You don't have to wait,\" I corrected. \"I can meet you at the door later, if you want help. Or you can go now.\"\n\n\"Later,\" they said. \"And please don't tell the man with the hook.\"\n\nMaui chose that precise moment to come through the window.\n\nNot the door. The window.\n\nHe had a string of damp rope over one shoulder and sand in his hair. One foot landed on the kitchen stool. The stool protested so loudly that the traveler flinched.\n\n\"Good news!\" Maui announced. \"I secured the—\"\n\n\"Window,\" I said.\n\n\"Very efficient route.\"\n\n\"Door.\"\n\n\"Slower route.\"\n\nThe traveler went white.\n\nI set the bread on the table and stepped between them and the window.\n\n\"Maui, I need you outside for a minute.\"\n\nHe looked at my face and lost the grin.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nThat was progress. Last month he would have asked five questions and answered three of them himself.\n\nAfter he climbed back out, I walked the traveler to the covered passage behind the bakery. They gave me the name they'd chosen for the arrival book: Lark. They said it softly, like a name they weren't certain they were permitted to keep.\n\nWe agreed on a meeting place near the eastern arch at dusk. They did not tell me why they were leaving, and I did not ask.\n\nWhen I returned, Maui was standing outside with the injured expression of a man denied the chance to make a heroic speech.\n\nHe kicked at a pebble with enough force to send it across the courtyard.\n\n\"Great. I made a stranger afraid of me without even getting to do anything heroic. New personal record.\"\n\n\"Not every stranger needs the hero,\" I said.\n\n\"Some don't even want to meet him.\" Maui looked genuinely offended by that possibility, then caught my expression. \"Okay, fine. Most people do. A few have terrible taste.\"\n\nMini Maui sat on his chest, made an exaggerated sad face, then kicked an imaginary door shut. Maui covered the tattoo before it could complete the pantomime.\n\n\"I wasn't going to follow Lark,\" he said. \"I just wanted to know whether there was a reason they were scared.\"\n\n\"That's their story.\"\n\n\"I know.\" He looked toward the covered passage and rubbed the back of his neck. \"I just wish knowing made it less personal.\"\n\nThe honesty lasted a heartbeat. Then he straightened to his full height and announced that he was going to find something very heavy to lift while he contemplated his many admirable qualities. He chose a sack of flour Mara had already asked someone to move. That was about as healthy a response as I expected from him that day.\n\n\"Did I scare someone?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I didn't know they were there.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe folded his arms. \"If someone needs to leave, I can escort them. I know every safe path from the cove.\"\n\n\"They explicitly asked me not to tell you.\"\n\nHis expression sharpened.\n\n\"Do they think I would hurt them?\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Then why—\"\n\n\"Because that's their choice.\"\n\nHe looked toward the bakery. A child was chasing a dropped apple across the square. The world went on around his silence.\n\n\"You think I can't be trusted,\" he said.\n\n\"I think you want to be useful. That's not the same thing as being invited.\"\n\nMaui turned away.\n\nI let him have the space.\n\nBy afternoon I had a list of problems. Some were obvious: Pip sometimes announced arrival names before asking permission. The arrival book had a column labeled EXPECTED RETURN, as though every departure were a promise. Neri kept a spare key for every door but hadn't written down who could request one. The eastern arch had a railing that blocked one side of the approach.\n\nThe least obvious problem was that everyone wanted to help in their own preferred way. Which meant that a traveler who needed privacy had to reject kindness five times before getting what they'd asked for.\n\nNeri listened while I explained.\n\nThey rested both hands over their walking stick.\n\n\"The register was meant to protect travelers. If someone went missing, we wanted to know which way to search.\"\n\n\"Search only if they've asked to be searched for,\" I said.\n\nNeri's eyes dimmed. \"That is a difficult thing to say after you've lost someone.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\nI thought of the door to my old life. All the people I couldn't name, some of whom might be looking for me. I thought of how sick I felt when someone assumed not remembering meant I belonged to whoever recognized me first.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"More than I'd like.\"\n\nNeri looked toward the arches.\n\n\"We used to close them in storms.\"\n\n\"Did the traveler agree?\"\n\nThey did not answer.\n\nThe silence bothered me enough to write it down.\n\nAt dusk I met Lark beside the eastern arch. The air smelled of wet stone and burned rosemary. They had one travel pack and the loaf of bread wrapped in cloth.\n\n\"Are you coming with me?\" they asked.\n\n\"Only as far as you want.\"\n\n\"Can you stand back while I open it?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI went to the railing and stood facing the square. Behind me a brass latch clicked. Cold air touched my neck.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\nI turned.\n\nLark was waiting in the doorway, their shoulders straight now.\n\n\"Thank you for not asking.\"\n\n\"Safe travels.\"\n\nThey smiled once and stepped through. The door closed.\n\nNothing terrible happened. No bell rang in protest. The lights at the well stayed lit.\n\nI found Maui at the cove checking the new tide chart. He had covered the little crab drawing with a square of parchment, then uncovered it again, as if even the crab's expression needed approval.\n\n\"They left,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"I saw the door light change.\" He paused. \"I stayed here.\"\n\nI leaned against the railing beside him.\n\n\"Was it hard?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI appreciated the answer.\n\nHe stared at the horizon. \"I've spent so much of my life proving I can get someone home. I never considered that getting out of the way might be the useful thing.\"\n\nI found his hand where it rested on the rail. He turned his palm up.\n\n\"I didn't tell you because Lark asked me not to,\" I said. \"Not because I don't trust you. Sometimes someone's privacy belongs to them even when keeping it makes us uncomfortable.\"\n\n\"I know that now.\" He glanced sideways. \"I might forget it.\"\n\n\"Then I'll remind you.\"\n\n\"You're very good at being annoying.\"\n\n\"I've been practicing.\"\n\nHe lifted my hand and kissed my knuckles. The gesture was so unexpected that warmth climbed up my neck.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked, close enough that his voice lowered.\n\n\"You may.\"\n\nHe kissed me gently beside the new tide sign. The sea moved beneath us. He did not promise to be perfect, and I did not need him to.\n\nThe following morning, Pip and I rewrote the arrival register. There would be an optional contact and a private note indicating whether someone wanted assistance leaving. Nobody would read names aloud without permission. And where the old page said EXPECTED RETURN, we wrote one question instead:\n\nDO YOU WANT SOMEONE TO WAIT FOR YOU?\n\nLark's row remained blank.\n\nFor the first time, I understood that a blank line could be an act of care.","word_count":1437},"maui-13":{"number":13,"title":"Ink on the Water","content":"The sea returned a piece of paper on a morning when nobody had asked it for anything.\n\nMaui found it caught between two stones below the repaired landing. He was looking for a replacement mooring ring, which was a sensible task. Finding a soaking-wet fragment of the Lanternweald's oldest ledger did not belong on his list.\n\n\"Treasure,\" he declared when he carried it up the steps.\n\n\"That is a piece of rubbish.\"\n\n\"Treasure frequently begins as rubbish to people with no imagination.\"\n\n\"It smells like fish.\"\n\n\"An authentic sea treasure.\"\n\nHe laid it on the workbench. The paper was thick, almost cloth, with an edge blackened by an old burn. Pale ink ran between two columns of dates. Most of the writing had dissolved.\n\nNear the bottom there was a drawing of a key.\n\nA brass key with a tiny half-moon notch.\n\nMy fingers moved to the blue thread at my throat before I could stop them.\n\nMaui noticed.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\nI pulled out my key and held it beside the drawing.\n\nHis grin vanished.\n\n\"Oh.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe leaned close, careful not to touch the damaged paper. The notch matched, right down to a small nick along the lower tooth.\n\n\"That means this was yours.\"\n\n\"Does it?\"\n\nHe straightened.\n\nI studied the drawing again. There was no name. No address. Nothing to prove the paper had been written about me rather than about whoever had owned a similar key before I arrived.\n\n\"It's a drawing,\" I said. \"We don't know who made it.\"\n\n\"But it looks exactly like—\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nMy voice came out sharper than I intended. Maui stepped back.\n\n\"I wasn't trying to decide for you.\"\n\n\"I know.\" I swallowed. \"I'm sorry.\"\n\nHe watched me, the hurt in his eyes giving way to worry.\n\n\"Do you want to put it away?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nWe dried the page between clean sheets in Tamsin's workshop. She weighed the corners with smooth stones and forbade Maui from breathing heroically near it.\n\nMaui hovered over the workbench until Tamsin pointed at a chalk line on the floor.\n\n\"Behind that.\"\n\n\"That line was drawn for children.\"\n\n\"Then you should understand it easily.\"\n\nI made a sound that came out halfway between a cough and a laugh. Mini Maui rolled down his arm, pressed his own palms to an imaginary pane of glass, and stared at the ledger like a tiny impatient museum visitor.\n\nMaui nudged him back toward his shoulder. \"I'm excellent at solving ancient mysteries.\"\n\n\"You're excellent at dropping sand on wet paper,\" Tamsin replied.\n\n\"One time.\"\n\n\"Today.\"\n\nHe threw his arms wide in injured magnificence, but his eyes kept returning to the torn corner of the ledger. People had once written down who was allowed to leave. No amount of muscle could make the missing words reappear.\n\nAfter several minutes he sat on a stool behind the line. He still whispered spectacular theories to me whenever Tamsin turned away. Some were nonsense. A few made me ask better questions. He wasn't suddenly a scholar; he was a curious trickster who hated a puzzle he couldn't wrestle.\n\n\"I don't breathe heroically.\"\n\n\"You are doing it now,\" she said.\n\nWe waited.\n\nThe sunlight moved across the room. Beyond the open shutter, people crossed Lanternwell Square carrying market baskets. The new departure register lay on Neri's desk. A child had already asked whether the crab on the tide sign was a guardian spirit.\n\nMaui had said yes.\n\nI had said no.\n\nThe child had decided it was a guardian spirit of checking the tide.\n\nBy noon, enough of the page had dried for Tamsin to lift it. A second drawing emerged beside the key: a stone arch with seven short scratches along its underside.\n\n\"The well has only six,\" I said.\n\nTamsin looked at me.\n\n\"Are you sure?\"\n\n\"I counted them when we hung the festival lanterns.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps you should count again.\"\n\nI disliked reasonable advice when it involved climbing ladders.\n\nNeri found us at the well.\n\nThey took one look at the page and went still.\n\nI had seen people frightened. This was different. They looked like someone whose childhood had come through the door without knocking.\n\n\"Where did you get that?\"\n\n\"At the cove,\" Maui said. \"Between the south stones.\"\n\nNeri held out their hands.\n\nI almost gave it to them immediately. It seemed kinder.\n\nThen I remembered Lark asking to leave without explanation. The way Neri had said we used to close them in storms and then refused to describe who could leave.\n\n\"Can we look at it together?\" I asked.\n\nNeri's hands fell.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe took the page to Moonroot Archive, where the workroom had a dry slate table and a window that faced the spring. A librarian brought a low lamp. Iroh, who had stopped by to return a borrowed history, offered tea and then, after looking at Neri's face, quietly left it on a side table.\n\nNo one turned the scene into a performance.\n\nNeri traced the burnt edge without touching it.\n\n\"This is from the old maintenance ledger.\"\n\n\"Why was it in the water?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not everything burned stays burned. There was a flood.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"Before the new Threadways.\"\n\n\"Before me?\"\n\nThey nodded.\n\nI turned the page slightly. Beneath the key was a number written in narrow, fading strokes. Twelve.\n\n\"Is that a date?\"\n\n\"No.\" Neri's voice grew quiet. \"A measurement. Twelve feet from the old waterline.\"\n\nMaui looked toward the window.\n\n\"The spring?\"\n\n\"Under it.\"\n\nThere was a long silence.\n\n\"So the key isn't necessarily mine,\" I said.\n\nNeri met my eyes.\n\n\"No. It belonged to people who maintained the drainage path.\"\n\n\"People, plural?\"\n\n\"More than one keeper held that pattern.\"\n\nRelief and disappointment arrived together. Both were uncomfortable. Neither was a reason to demand a different answer.\n\n\"Why would the drains be locked?\" Maui asked.\n\nNeri looked down at their walking stick.\n\n\"Because someone was afraid of what might happen if a door stayed open.\"\n\nA thin current of anger went through me.\n\n\"Which door?\"\n\nNeri did not answer.\n\nI could have pushed. Part of me wanted to. I'd spent so long with gaps in my memory that any missing piece felt personal. A scrap of paper with my key on it seemed like an invitation to make the entire valley's history about myself.\n\nInstead, I placed the page between two protective sheets.\n\n\"We should record what we actually know,\" I said. \"Found at the cove. Drawing of a maintenance key. Old waterline. An arch that doesn't match the one outside.\"\n\nMaui leaned against the table.\n\n\"And what we don't know?\"\n\n\"That too.\"\n\nNeri looked at me for a long moment. Then they nodded.\n\n\"I can tell you where the older diagrams are kept,\" they said. \"I am not ready to tell you everything that happened.\"\n\n\"That's your choice,\" I answered. \"But we need to know whether the old structure is dangerous.\"\n\n\"It may be.\"\n\n\"Then we need people qualified to inspect it, whether you're ready for the story or not.\"\n\nNeri's face changed. Not pleased, exactly. More like someone recognizing a door they'd hoped never to open.\n\nOutside the archive, Maui and I walked along the canal. The stones were warm. A few late flowers clung to the cracks between them.\n\n\"You're angry,\" he said.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"At Neri?\"\n\n\"At the part where everybody's safety depends on a story no one wants to tell.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Would you have wanted them to say it all?\"\n\n\"Yes. No. I don't know.\" I touched the key again. \"I want the past to make sense. But I don't want the past to own me.\"\n\nMaui stopped beside the bridge.\n\n\"You know what I think?\"\n\n\"I anticipate a very dramatic answer.\"\n\n\"I think you can be curious without being a prisoner.\"\n\n\"That was suspiciously reasonable.\"\n\n\"I occasionally produce wisdom by accident.\"\n\nHe took my hand. My thumb followed a scar across his knuckles. His expression softened, and he stepped closer.\n\n\"Still want to look at the drawings tomorrow?\" he asked.\n\n\"With proper tools, yes.\"\n\n\"Still want me there?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe kissed my forehead. For a moment the canal and the lost ledger and the question of who first held my key became small enough to carry.\n\nThat evening I went back to the well alone and counted the scratches.\n\nOne. Two. Three. Four. Five. Six.\n\nThe seventh was not scratched into the arch.\n\nIt was hidden on the underside of a stone below the lantern hooks, visible only when I crouched and looked from the water's edge.\n\nI did not pry it open. I did not call for Maui to lift the stone with heroic ease.\n\nI drew what I saw and wrote down the angle of the light.\n\nThen I went home and put my key beside my notebook.\n\nSome mysteries were worth approaching carefully.\n\nThat did not mean they would wait forever.","word_count":1479},"maui-14":{"number":14,"title":"The Quiet Work of Rope","content":"The rope snapped with a sound like somebody cracking a whip across the shore.\n\nI saw Maui's hand jerk backward. The mooring post shuddered. A boat that had been safely tucked against the Tideglass landing swung toward the rocks, and the two children sitting inside it screamed.\n\n\"Maui!\"\n\nHe was already moving.\n\nHe caught the bow with his hook before the boat could strike the stone. His feet planted in the wet sand, knees bent, and for a moment the whole dock seemed to strain against him. Then Fen reached the line, dragged it through the remaining ring, and tied off the boat while I helped the children onto the steps.\n\nThey were frightened. They were unhurt.\n\nMaui was still smiling when I turned toward him.\n\n\"See?\" he said. \"Everyone's fine.\"\n\nHis left hand was bleeding.\n\n\"Show me.\"\n\n\"It's a scratch.\"\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHe held it up. There was a raw stripe across the base of his palm, deeper at one end where the rope had caught. He flexed his fingers and tried to hide the wince under a grin.\n\n\"Hero's scratch.\"\n\n\"Well, the hero is going to sit down.\"\n\nFen looked at the broken line and swore softly.\n\n\"That shouldn't have given way.\"\n\nI checked the end. One part was frayed, but a second section looked almost polished. The strand had been rubbing against the underside of the landing, where a nail had shifted during the recent repairs.\n\n\"Maintenance failure,\" I said.\n\nFen nodded.\n\n\"Not a monster?\"\n\nMaui looked mildly offended.\n\n\"I could still fight one.\"\n\n\"Try fighting a clean bandage first.\"\n\nThe clinic didn't open until noon, but Katara had left an emergency kit in the public storage cabinet. I made Maui sit on an overturned crate while I cleaned the cut. He watched with an expression of tremendous patience that lasted perhaps thirty seconds.\n\n\"I could heal faster as a hawk.\"\n\n\"Do hawks have hands?\"\n\n\"Not technically.\"\n\n\"Then stay as you are.\"\n\nHe examined the wound over my shoulder.\n\n\"I've had worse.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\n\"Much worse.\"\n\n\"Still believe you.\"\n\n\"That time with the eel—\"\n\n\"If you tell me this cut is nothing compared to an eel, I am going to make the bandage extremely decorative.\"\n\nHe shut his mouth.\n\nFen took the broken line to Tamsin's workshop. The children went home with Mara, who offered bread and a stern lecture about staying seated while docking. By the time I finished the bandage, the tide had begun to turn.\n\nMaui reached for his hook.\n\n\"No,\" I said.\n\nHe stared at me.\n\n\"No?\"\n\n\"Not until we know whether that hand needs proper treatment. And not until somebody else examines the rest of the moorings.\"\n\n\"I can inspect them.\"\n\n\"I know you can. That's not the problem.\"\n\n\"Then what is?\"\n\nI wanted to say that he was exhausting. I wanted to say that I couldn't bear the thought of him being hurt again, because the sight of blood on his hand had made something awful open inside my chest.\n\nNeither would explain why the dock had failed.\n\n\"The problem,\" I said carefully, \"is that everyone assumes you're the solution to every emergency. Even you.\"\n\nFor a long moment he said nothing.\n\nThen he looked toward the children disappearing into the square.\n\n\"They were scared.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"And I could help.\"\n\n\"You did help. Now other people get to help too.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bandage.\n\n\"Doesn't feel right.\"\n\nThat was the most honest thing he'd said all morning.\n\nAt Tamsin's workshop, Fen showed us three knots with different jobs. One held under steady weight, one could be untied after a sudden pull, and the third was designed so a damaged line could be replaced without anyone putting a hand where the load would crush it.\n\nI got the first one wrong twice.\n\nMaui got it right immediately, then tried to demonstrate with his injured hand.\n\nMini Maui appeared at his wrist and performed an entire play about a heroic injured hand. It ended with the miniature collapsing dramatically beside a rope.\n\n\"I know,\" Maui said. \"I'm suffering.\"\n\n\"You're showing off,\" I said.\n\n\"Two things can be true.\"\n\nFen pulled the line toward them and pointed to the section that would carry the actual load. Maui leaned closer, interested despite himself. He'd tied enough lines in his life to make the motions look effortless, but he was watching how Fen tested this particular wet rope.\n\n\"Is this how all of you pass the time?\" he asked. \"Inventing ways to make a demigod feel inadequate?\"\n\n\"Mostly we repair the damage caused by people who never check their knots.\"\n\nMaui looked wounded. Then he grinned and offered his uninjured hand.\n\n\"Fine. Tell me what the legendary Maui has to do to pass your tiny rope exam.\"\n\nHe listened to the answer. His hand was shaking by the end, partly from the cut and partly from how determined he was not to be the person who made the next boat unsafe. The grin stayed. So did the effort.\n\nFen slapped the rope away.\n\n\"Use the other one.\"\n\n\"You're very demanding for a person teaching knots.\"\n\n\"You're very loud for somebody who hasn't paid the repair bill.\"\n\nMaui blinked.\n\n\"There's a bill?\"\n\n\"There is always a bill.\"\n\nFen pointed to the ledger on the wall. Rope cost money. Nails cost money. Tamsin's time cost money. So did replacement planks. The Lanternweald's dock fees paid for some things, but not everything, and the people who kept boats floating weren't decorative figures in somebody else's epic.\n\nWe all looked at the ledger.\n\nTwo weeks of repairs had nearly emptied the landing fund.\n\n\"Why didn't you tell us?\" I asked.\n\n\"Because half the village offers to help for free and the other half feels guilty about not being able to,\" Fen said. \"I need the tools paid for. I don't need a parade.\"\n\nMaui was quiet.\n\nI wondered whether he was considering selling a legend to cover the price of nails.\n\nInstead he asked, \"How much for the rope?\"\n\nFen named an amount.\n\n\"I can pay some,\" Maui said.\n\n\"You don't owe the whole dock because you were here when it failed.\"\n\n\"I know. But I use it.\"\n\nFen looked surprised.\n\n\"That's different.\"\n\nI spent the afternoon helping inspect the mooring points. We marked each one with colored thread, not to rank them but to show which were safe, which needed maintenance, and which were closed until tested. Pip offered to translate the signs for travelers who couldn't read the Lanternweald script. Toph arrived and tapped the planks with her heel.\n\n\"Wood's talking,\" she announced.\n\n\"Does it have good news?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it's begging to be replaced.\"\n\nShe marked two supports we'd missed.\n\nMaui hated staying on the bench. He was especially offended when a small child offered him a cup of water and called him brave for sitting still.\n\n\"That was sarcastic,\" he said after the child left.\n\n\"That was generous.\"\n\n\"You're enjoying this.\"\n\n\"Immensely.\"\n\nBy sunset the dock was closed, the replacement list was posted, and nobody had suffered another injury.\n\nI found Maui alone at the end of the cove. He held a short length of spare rope in his good hand, turning it over as if it were a puzzle.\n\n\"Fen taught me something,\" he said.\n\n\"A knot?\"\n\n\"No. Well, yes. But something else.\" He pulled one loop through another. \"When I was younger, people always wanted proof I was strong. Then when I became strong, they wanted proof I could do anything. I got used to being the person nobody had to ask.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"Does it frighten you to be asked to stop?\"\n\nHe took a long time to answer.\n\n\"It frightens me that if I stop, you might discover you never needed me.\"\n\nThe words made me ache.\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHe looked at me with an openness so unlike his stage voice that I barely recognized it.\n\n\"I need you,\" I said. \"But not because you can hold a boat against the sea. I need the person who remembered I hate my tea too sweet. The one who painted a crab on a safety sign and then made sure it was the correct kind of crab.\"\n\n\"It is a very accurate crab.\"\n\n\"The most accurate.\"\n\nHe laughed once.\n\n\"I need you to tell me when you're scared,\" I continued. \"And I need you to let somebody else hold a rope when you're hurt.\"\n\nHe looked at his bandage.\n\n\"Even if they're bad at knots?\"\n\n\"Especially then. They can learn.\"\n\nHe looped his good arm around my shoulders. I leaned against him. His cheek brushed my hair, warm from the sun, and the smell of salt and timber rose between us.\n\n\"I could teach you the next knot,\" he murmured.\n\n\"Tomorrow. Tonight you're coming home.\"\n\n\"Bossy.\"\n\n\"You like it.\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nHe kissed me, slow enough that I could feel his smile become something gentler. We went home before the tide rose.\n\nThe following morning, the broken mooring was still broken. The ledger still needed funds. But Fen had drawn a new maintenance rota, Tamsin had priced the supplies, and Maui had signed up for a shift he could do with one hand.\n\nNobody owed him applause.\n\nHe seemed relieved.","word_count":1541},"maui-15":{"number":15,"title":"No One Is a Prize","content":"The visitor made the remark over a platter of roasted mushrooms.\n\nHe had come from a distant island, arrived through the Tideglass Door, and spent most of the afternoon telling anyone who would listen about a sea voyage I suspected grew longer each time he described it.\n\nMaui knew him. That was the problem.\n\n\"Biggest fish I've ever seen,\" the visitor said, stretching both arms wide.\n\nMaui grinned. \"You've forgotten the one that nearly ate your boat.\"\n\n\"That was a different fish!\"\n\nThey laughed. I laughed too. It was easy, for a while, to enjoy the sound of two people remembering a life that existed before I knew either of them.\n\nThen the visitor turned toward me.\n\n\"And you must be his reward,\" he said.\n\nI thought I'd misheard.\n\n\"Excuse me?\"\n\n\"For all his heroic deeds! The gods finally sent him someone worth keeping. Maui always did know how to win a prize.\"\n\nThe platter stayed warm in my hands.\n\nAround us, the Lanternwell feast continued. Mara was arguing with a customer about the price of honey. Tamsin had brought her repaired sixth table. Iroh was pouring tea, and Greg had taken his guitar out only long enough to tune it.\n\nMaui laughed.\n\nNot cruelly. Not even deliberately. It was an old reflex, the easy bark of somebody who had heard a hundred boasts and knew how to answer them.\n\nIt still hurt.\n\nI put the platter down.\n\n\"I'm not a prize,\" I said.\n\nThe visitor blinked.\n\n\"Only a compliment.\"\n\n\"It isn't one.\"\n\nHe looked at Maui, as though Maui could explain why the property had started speaking.\n\n\"Come now, surely you know what I meant.\"\n\n\"I know what you said.\"\n\nMaui stopped smiling.\n\nFor a moment I saw every muscle in his shoulders bunch. The hook on his back gleamed where the lamplight caught its curve. I knew the posture: the great Maui was about to make an example of someone.\n\nMini Maui planted his tiny feet against his chest and shook his head so violently that the tattooed hair bounced.\n\n\"What?\" Maui whispered.\n\nThe miniature pointed to me instead of the visitor.\n\nMaui exhaled through his nose. His grin came back, but it was sharp at the edges.\n\n\"Friend,\" he told the man, \"I have fought things larger and uglier than that sentence. Don't make me waste a perfectly good feast on it.\"\n\nI felt my own hands shake with anger. Maui saw that and took a step back, leaving me space to answer. He wasn't suddenly calm. He was furious enough to split a serving platter. He simply wasn't making my objection disappear inside his heroic rescue.\n\nLater, when we had a moment alone, he admitted he had laughed because he'd liked being congratulated. He didn't try to dress the mistake up as a noble misunderstanding.\n\n\"The applause gets to me,\" he said. \"Doesn't make it right.\"\n\nThe visitor waved a hand.\n\n\"Don't make a storm over a small phrase. You look happy together. That's all.\"\n\n\"Then you can say we look happy together.\"\n\nThe words came out steady. I was pleased about that, because my hands had begun to shake.\n\nMaui opened his mouth.\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Please don't smooth this over for him.\"\n\nHe closed it.\n\nSilence reached our corner of the table, then broke around it as people resumed conversations. The visitor's expression hardened with embarrassment.\n\n\"I was teasing.\"\n\n\"I heard you.\"\n\n\"Your partner doesn't seem offended.\"\n\nMaui put his cup down.\n\n\"My partner can speak for themself,\" he said.\n\nI appreciated that, but I still wanted to ask him why he had laughed. Not here. Not while the visitor was looking for an audience. The question belonged between us.\n\nThe man took his drink and went to find another table.\n\nI carried the mushrooms to the serving bench. Mara accepted the platter and gave me a look I couldn't quite read.\n\n\"Would you like to sit in the back for a minute?\" she asked.\n\n\"No, thank you.\"\n\n\"Good. I was going to ask you to slice bread.\"\n\nI almost smiled.\n\nShe handed me a knife. We worked together while the celebration moved around us, a small pocket of ordinary labor that didn't require me to explain how I felt.\n\nAfter a while Maui came beside the table.\n\n\"Can I help?\"\n\nMara pointed him toward the bread baskets.\n\n\"You can carry those to the other end without turning it into a victory march.\"\n\n\"I have never turned bread into a victory march.\"\n\n\"There's still time.\"\n\nHe obeyed.\n\nWe didn't speak privately until the last plates were collected and the lanterns began to dim. The visitor had already left. Nobody had chased him away, but nobody had offered a second audience either.\n\nMaui found me at the well.\n\n\"You were angry,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"With him?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe paused.\n\n\"With me?\"\n\nI turned to him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe flinched, just a little.\n\n\"I didn't mean to laugh at you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"He says things like that. Always has. We used to make it into a competition, who had the biggest tale or the best ending. I heard the joke before I heard the words.\"\n\n\"I could tell.\"\n\nHe looked miserable.\n\nPart of me wanted to comfort him immediately. I cared about him. Seeing him upset still made me want to reach for his hand. But if I rescued every conversation before he'd understood the problem, neither of us would learn much.\n\n\"Tell me what you heard after I spoke,\" I said.\n\nHe leaned on the railing.\n\n\"That you aren't something I won. That nobody sent you as a reward. That you're here because you chose to be.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked toward the arches.\n\n\"And I shouldn't have laughed.\"\n\n\"You can laugh at an old joke and still realize it wasn't fair. I'm not asking you to become someone who never gets things wrong. I'm asking you to notice when the joke makes me smaller.\"\n\nMaui nodded slowly.\n\n\"I thought if I fought him in front of everyone, it would turn into a performance.\"\n\n\"It might have.\"\n\n\"I thought staying quiet meant I was protecting you.\"\n\n\"Sometimes people need support. Sometimes they need space. You can ask.\"\n\nHe touched the bandage on his hand, now smaller than before.\n\n\"Like the rope.\"\n\n\"Like the rope.\"\n\nThere was a pause long enough for the square to settle into evening. Someone rang the last market bell. A cart rattled past the bakery, its wheels catching on the seam between stones.\n\n\"Do you want me to speak to him?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"Not on my behalf. I said what I needed.\"\n\n\"And if he does it again?\"\n\n\"Then we tell him he can't keep insulting people at our table. Together.\"\n\nHe considered that.\n\n\"You know,\" he said, \"I once stole a whole island for somebody I thought I had to impress.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"You did what?\"\n\n\"Long story.\"\n\n\"I imagine the island found it long too.\"\n\nHe grinned, briefly. Then he grew serious.\n\n\"You make me think about what I call mine.\"\n\nI touched his wrist.\n\n\"You can call me your partner. When I agree to it.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"And I can call you mine in the same way.\"\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nI stepped closer. His hand found the small of my back, not possessive, simply steady. He waited as I tilted my face toward him.\n\n\"You don't have to earn me with a legend,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"I'm trying.\"\n\nI kissed him. The first touch was almost shy. He answered with a warmth that made my knees briefly forget their job. When we drew apart, I rested my forehead against his, and we laughed softly at the absurdity of two people who could argue about language and still become hopelessly distracted by each other.\n\nAt home, we ate the last of Mara's bread and talked about the first time we'd each been treated as somebody else's story.\n\nMaui told me about people praising his strength without ever learning what frightened him. I told him how much I hated the assumption that my missing memories made me easy to define.\n\nHe listened without trying to fix it.\n\nThe candles burned low. He brushed his thumb along my palm and asked whether I wanted him to stay.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said.\n\nThe kitchen grew quiet. We closed the door and left the rest of the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning, a small note had appeared beneath the lantern hooks.\n\nThe visitor had left an apology, awkwardly phrased, with no request that I accept it. Maui showed it to me but did not tell me what to feel.\n\nI folded it and placed it in my notebook.\n\nAt the next feast, I would speak my own name.\n\nThat should never have needed to be a victory.\n\nIt still felt like one.","word_count":1471},"maui-16":{"number":16,"title":"The Sea Glass Orchard","content":"Tamsin had drawn the orchard in blue pencil because she thought the blue made the argument more convincing.\n\nShe unfolded the plan across the kitchen table and weighted the corners with four stones. Outside, the tide was retreating, leaving dark streaks on the repaired dock. Maui was carving something into the end of a broom handle. He said it was a decorative fish. It looked like a fish trying to escape a terrible situation.\n\n\"An orchard?\" I asked.\n\n\"A sheltered growing space,\" Tamsin corrected. \"Don't tell people I've promised trees.\"\n\nThere were six raised beds on her sketch, two narrow paths, a low wall of reclaimed stone, and a series of open channels leading away from the beach. The paths curved around the old signal post rather than through it.\n\n\"Why the channels?\"\n\n\"Saltwater doesn't care how pretty your lavender is. If a wave comes over the barrier, I want it to leave again.\"\n\nMaui examined the drawing.\n\n\"I could build you a wall high enough to stop any wave.\"\n\n\"Then you'd stop the wind,\" Tamsin said. \"And the rain would collect behind it. And the marsh birds would lose their landing.\"\n\n\"Ah.\"\n\n\"That's one problem with high walls. They solve the thing you can see.\"\n\nI traced a finger around the empty beds.\n\n\"We could invite everyone to plant something.\"\n\nTamsin gave me a long look.\n\n\"Everyone?\"\n\n\"People have gardens in their homeworlds. We could make this a place to share cuttings.\"\n\n\"Would you put a mountain fern next to a saltmarsh reed?\"\n\n\"I'd ask somebody who knows plants.\"\n\n\"Good. I'm somebody.\"\n\nShe turned the sketch over. On the back was a list of species and soil conditions: sea kale, beach peas, saltgrass, two varieties of hardy lavender, and a climbing vine from the sheltered slopes. Alongside each name she'd written where its roots belonged, what water it tolerated, and how far it might spread.\n\nI noticed three names crossed out.\n\n\"Why not these?\"\n\n\"Because one drinks too much freshwater, one strangles everything it climbs, and one is beautiful for a season before its roots crack the stone.\"\n\nI thought of the way people talked about Lanternweald as though its doors meant they could bring any piece of home through and set it wherever they wished.\n\n\"Then we need a steward,\" I said.\n\n\"We have one.\"\n\nShe pointed out the window.\n\nFen was kneeling beside the brackish pool, checking the tide mark with a ruler. They had been maintaining the coastal channels long before anybody thought to make them fashionable.\n\n\"Have you asked Fen what they want?\" Tamsin said.\n\nI had not.\n\nMaui, who had been listening very carefully while pretending not to, put down his unfinished fish.\n\n\"I could ask.\"\n\nTamsin smiled without mercy.\n\n\"One of you should.\"\n\nWe went to the shore.\n\nFen listened to the orchard idea with the expression of someone hearing that a beloved relative wanted to keep six goats in the sitting room.\n\n\"People want to plant,\" I explained.\n\n\"People always want to plant. Nobody wants to pull up dead stems in February.\"\n\n\"We'll make a rota.\"\n\n\"Who checks the water salinity? Who notices when the drainage channel silts over? Who tells a visitor their favorite plant can't survive here?\"\n\nI looked at the sand beneath my boots.\n\n\"That last one might have to be me.\"\n\nFen raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"You were going to invite everyone.\"\n\n\"I was going to invite everyone to contribute,\" I said, feeling the distinction form as I spoke. \"Not invite everyone to do whatever they please.\"\n\n\"Better.\"\n\nThey led us around the old signal post. The ground dipped into a sheltered crescent, protected from the strongest wind by a ridge of stone. Tiny white flowers grew between patches of saltgrass.\n\n\"This is a nursery already,\" Fen said. \"The plants are small because they've been fighting to stay here. Dig all of them up and you'll spend two years trying to replace what you didn't notice.\"\n\nMaui crouched beside the flowers.\n\nHis enormous shadow covered half the tiny plants. Mini Maui sprang across his chest and raised both hands as though he could block it.\n\n\"Look at that,\" Maui said. \"The bravest little army in Tideglass. No weapons. No training. Not even the sense to flee a giant with a fishhook.\"\n\n\"They've been here longer than we have,\" Fen said.\n\n\"Then they're clearly in charge.\"\n\nHe scooped a handful of loose sand from beside the path and let it fall slowly through his fingers, watching how the wind carried the grains. He wanted to move the whole ridge. I could almost hear the plan forming behind his eyes.\n\n\"Don't,\" I said before he opened his mouth.\n\n\"I haven't even suggested anything.\"\n\n\"You're thinking about moving a hill.\"\n\n\"Only a *small* hill.\"\n\nThe tattoo slapped its forehead. Maui sighed theatrically and stood where he wouldn't block the sun. His pride was still loud enough to hear across the garden. But he stayed until Fen had finished explaining which part of the sheltered ground could actually be planted. The flowers didn't need an island-maker. They needed the wind left alone.\n\n\"They're brave.\"\n\n\"They're plants,\" Fen replied.\n\n\"Brave plants.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nFen wasn't smiling.\n\n\"They keep the sand from moving. That's their job. The orchard needs to work with them.\"\n\nWe walked the ground, marked where water actually flowed, and drew a revised plan. The best place for the raised beds was smaller than Tamsin's sketch. The old nursery would remain untouched. Paths needed to be wide enough for a handcart and for people who couldn't step down over the rocks. We would use soil already available from a failed market planter rather than hauling peat from another world.\n\nThe work didn't look as impressive.\n\nIt looked possible.\n\nBy afternoon, three more people had arrived to offer their opinions. Mara wanted edible herbs for the kitchen. Iroh had a small cutting of rosemary that needed more sun than his terrace offered. Pip wanted a bed of flowers for the Lantern Walk. Edda wanted a resting bench where the path bent toward the shore.\n\nI wrote every suggestion down.\n\nMaui looked over my shoulder.\n\n\"You're making a book of wishes.\"\n\n\"Then tomorrow we compare it to the book of water.\"\n\n\"Less romantic.\"\n\n\"Much more useful.\"\n\nWe held the first gathering by the signal post at dusk. Fen explained which species were suited to the salt spray. Tamsin showed the revised layout. When someone proposed a little grove of fruit trees, another resident explained the roots would reach the drainage stones. Nobody shouted. Somebody looked disappointed. That was allowed.\n\nMara brought a basket of bread, which helped.\n\nThe group agreed to trial three raised beds first. One would hold edible coastal plants, one would be a small nursery for hardy flowers, and one would test the dry-slope herbs. Everything would be labeled with the plant's water needs and origin, not just its pretty name. A spring review would decide whether to add more.\n\nAfter the meeting, I remained behind to pick up the sketches.\n\nMaui stood beside the old signal post, looking at the dark water.\n\n\"You wanted something bigger,\" he said.\n\n\"I did.\"\n\n\"Disappointed?\"\n\n\"A little.\"\n\nHe considered that.\n\n\"That's odd. I thought you loved gardens.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Then why does a smaller one make you sad?\"\n\nI looked at the three little rectangles marked on the sand.\n\n\"Because I wanted everyone to feel welcome. I imagined that meant saying yes to every idea.\"\n\nHe leaned against the post.\n\n\"People used to say yes to everything I promised. I thought they liked me.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"They liked the promise. Then I had to become big enough to keep it.\"\n\nI folded the sketches.\n\n\"You're getting annoyingly wise.\"\n\n\"I've had excellent instruction.\"\n\n\"Don't tell me where.\"\n\nHe grinned and took my hand.\n\nThe wind had grown cool. It smelled of wet stone and the distant bakery chimney. Maui rubbed his thumb over my knuckles and waited until I looked at him.\n\n\"Can I offer one thing that doesn't have to fit the plan?\" he asked.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"A bench. Just one. Where somebody can sit without feeling obliged to talk.\"\n\nI pictured Edda's proposed resting place.\n\n\"That absolutely fits the plan.\"\n\n\"Then I'm a genius.\"\n\n\"Let's not get carried away.\"\n\nHe kissed my forehead, then the corner of my mouth. The warmth of him against the evening air made it difficult to remember the precise dimensions of a raised bed.\n\n\"Come home,\" I said.\n\n\"Gladly.\"\n\nThe next morning, Fen showed us where to set the first stones. Maui carried them without making a speech. I planted a sprig of saltgrass at the edge of the new path and found the ground colder than I expected.\n\nBy the time the sun was up, we had one bed, two drainage channels, and no promise of a perfect orchard.\n\nIt was the most honest garden I'd ever helped begin.","word_count":1480},"maui-17":{"number":17,"title":"A Lantern for the Absent","content":"I couldn't decide what name to write on the lantern.\n\nThat was the trouble. There were names I remembered, names I had learned again, and names that lived just beyond my reach like objects at the bottom of dark water. If I wrote one, the others felt abandoned. If I wrote them all, I would have to admit how many empty spaces remained.\n\nMara watched me stare at the paper.\n\n\"You're allowed to leave it blank,\" she said.\n\n\"Everyone else is writing something.\"\n\n\"Everyone else is doing what they want.\"\n\nShe dipped her brush into a little pot of amber paint and continued a pattern of waves around her lantern. She had written nothing on it either.\n\n\"Who is yours for?\" I asked.\n\n\"Somebody who hated being named in public.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"Then why bring it?\"\n\n\"Because I still want to carry a light.\"\n\nThat answer stayed with me.\n\nFrostturn wasn't quite winter in Lanternweald. The weather didn't obey any one homeworld's calendar. The name belonged to a local stretch of short days, when the first hard frost reached the canal reeds and the western wind brought the smell of cold seaweed into Lanternwell Square. The Lantern Walk at Frostturn had once been a way for travelers to find their doors before night overtook the paths. Nowadays the district stewards still checked the crossings, but people also carried lights for memory, gratitude, mourning, or no reason at all.\n\nPip had painted signs showing the accessible route. Edda and Tamsin had marked the stretches where the cobbles were uneven. Danse had tested the pier lamps, and Iroh had insisted on hot tea for anyone who needed to stop along the way.\n\nNobody had to dedicate a lantern to anyone.\n\nI knew this. I had helped write the notice.\n\nKnowing wasn't the same as believing it for myself.\n\nMaui found me at the table after the others had gone to collect the lantern poles. He was carrying a basket of spare wicks and wearing a cloak with a dramatic fur collar.\n\n\"I bring supplies,\" he announced, lifting the wicker basket like a trophy. \"For what I assume is an extremely sophisticated lantern-making ceremony.\"\n\n\"It's paper and string.\"\n\n\"And bravery. Don't undersell bravery.\"\n\nMini Maui had somehow acquired an imaginary fur collar of his own. He strutted across the larger tattooed chest until Maui pressed a finger against the tiny figure and told it to behave.\n\nI smiled despite the knot in my throat.\n\nHis expression softened a fraction. \"Hey. I don't know who you're writing to. I don't have to.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"But if you'd like company, my company is famously exceptional.\"\n\n\"And if I want to sit here without anyone talking?\"\n\nHe looked aghast. \"An unfair test of my greatest weakness.\"\n\nI laughed. He sat anyway, made a show of sealing his lips, and lasted perhaps forty seconds before whispering that he'd defeated a storage cabinet that morning. Then he caught himself, shrugged, and pushed the basket toward me. I didn't need him to become silent or saintly. I needed him to recognize that the unnamed people I mourned were real, even when he couldn't put himself in their story.\n\n\"How many enemies have you defeated?\" I asked.\n\n\"Today? None.\"\n\n\"Tragic.\"\n\n\"I did battle with a stubborn storage cabinet.\"\n\n\"Did you win?\"\n\n\"It surrendered two hinges and a spider.\"\n\nHe put the basket down and looked at my blank lantern.\n\n\"Too many names?\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe didn't ask which ones.\n\nHe sat beside me and threaded a wick through a little brass ring. His injured hand had healed enough for ordinary work, though Fen still wouldn't let him touch the heaviest mooring ropes.\n\nAfter a while he said, \"I used to tell stories where everyone remembered me.\"\n\n\"That's not a surprise.\"\n\n\"No. But I thought if I made the story big enough, nobody would forget the people I'd lost along the way. I could keep them with me by talking.\"\n\n\"Did it work?\"\n\n\"For a while. Then I realized some people wanted to be remembered quietly.\"\n\nI ran my thumb over the paper.\n\n\"Some of mine might not even know I'm here.\"\n\n\"Do you want to go looking for them?\"\n\n\"Someday. Maybe. Not because tonight tells me I have to.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI picked up the brush and painted a small circle in the center of the lantern. I left a gap at the top, an opening too small for a name but large enough for light.\n\n\"That's it?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"For now.\"\n\n\"It's beautiful.\"\n\n\"You haven't seen it lit.\"\n\n\"I know what I like.\"\n\nI smiled, then caught myself worrying that I had been unfair to him. He had listened to my grief so patiently, and I wasn't sure how to explain that loving him did not close the spaces I'd carried from before.\n\n\"Maui?\"\n\n\"Hm?\"\n\n\"Can I ask you something awkward?\"\n\n\"I thrive on awkward questions.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"Absolutely not. Ask anyway.\"\n\nI put down the brush.\n\n\"You know I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I've told you that from the beginning, and I haven't changed my mind.\"\n\nHis expression remained open.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry that when I miss people, you'll think I'm wishing you were someone else.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern.\n\n\"I do wonder whether I'll be enough for the stories you haven't remembered.\"\n\n\"That's not a fair job to give you.\"\n\n\"I know.\" He hesitated. \"But being a hero makes it very tempting to think every problem needs me.\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"You don't need to replace anyone. You don't need to be the only person I love. I want you here because you're Maui, and because I choose you.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\n\"And if you choose another person too?\"\n\n\"Then I tell the truth. I don't expect you to agree to something just because it makes sense to me. We talk about boundaries, and you get to decide what you want.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a moment.\n\n\"I want to be someone you can come home to,\" he said. \"Without making home a cage.\"\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\n\"That's what I want too.\"\n\nWe stayed at the table until the lanterns were ready. Then Mara called for help, and the square became a pleasant disaster of people trying to carry hot drinks, spare wicks, and weatherproof coats at the same time.\n\nIroh arrived with a kettle. Greg had brought a small hand drum so people could keep pace without turning the walk into a concert. Toph tested the route by feeling the ground beneath her boots, and Aang helped the children hang lanterns where they could reach them safely.\n\nMaui carried my lantern.\n\nHe didn't ask what it meant.\n\nThe procession began at Lanternwell. The bells rang once, soft and ordinary. Along the canal, the lights stretched into a line that seemed to float just above the water. Frost had caught in the reeds, making them shine at their edges.\n\nI walked beside Maui past Jasmine Steps, then toward the cove.\n\nA woman ahead of us stopped at a bench. She held a lantern painted with three small stars. Her face was wet. Nobody asked her to explain. Edda moved a cup of tea within reach, and the woman nodded her thanks.\n\nIt occurred to me that privacy could be a kind of hospitality. Not silence forced on someone who needed to speak, but room for the choice.\n\nAt the water, Maui lifted my lantern toward a hook beneath the signal post.\n\n\"Here?\"\n\n\"Here.\"\n\nHe hung it carefully.\n\nThe flame caught the circle I'd painted, spilling light through the little gap.\n\nFor one terrible, beautiful moment I wanted to tell him every name. I wanted to invent the names I couldn't remember, if only to fill the absence.\n\nInstead, I leaned against him and whispered, \"I miss them.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe didn't ask who.\n\nI turned my face into his shoulder. His hand settled at the back of my coat, warm and steady.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" he asked quietly.\n\nI nodded.\n\nHis lips met mine beneath the lantern, gentle enough that grief could remain in the room without being crowded out. I kissed him again because I wanted to, not because it made the night less sad.\n\nLater we walked home after most of the lights had been extinguished. There were still several hanging along the safe route, each marked with a steward's name so no one would assume somebody else had put them out.\n\nAt our door, Maui paused.\n\n\"Do you want company?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And quiet?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\n\"I can do mostly.\"\n\nI laughed, tired and grateful. We sat with a blanket and a small bowl of warmed bread until the fire settled into coals.\n\nThe lantern at Tideglass burned until dawn.\n\nIt held no names.\n\nIt did not forget anyone.","word_count":1474},"maui-18":{"number":18,"title":"One Door Closed by Choice","content":"The door to my old room was still there.\n\nI had avoided it for three days after the Lantern Walk, which was an impressive achievement considering it stood only a few turns from the square. It was an ordinary painted door set into a stretch of old stone. The Threadway did not advertise where it led. Only the small brass plate with my chosen name marked it as mine.\n\nMaui stopped beside me.\n\n\"Do you want me to come?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI reached for the handle.\n\nThe brass was cold. When I turned it, a familiar smell met me: laundry soap, paper, and the faint dust of a room that had not been lived in for a while. The desk was where I remembered it. A book lay facedown beside a cup. The light came through the window at an angle I recognized without knowing why.\n\nThen I noticed the notebook.\n\nIt sat beneath the lamp, bound in faded green cloth, with a ribbon I could have tied myself. I knew the handwriting on the first page before I read the words.\n\nI had written a list of things I was afraid to ask for.\n\nThe first was simple: a day when I didn't have to explain myself.\n\nThe second had been crossed out so hard that the paper was nearly torn.\n\nThe third said: Don't let people decide where I belong just because they remember more than I do.\n\nI sat on the edge of the bed.\n\nI had expected the room to give me answers. Instead it gave me evidence of a person who had been frightened long before Lanternweald.\n\nFootsteps sounded in the hall behind me.\n\nMaui did not come through the door.\n\n\"Jasper?\" he called.\n\n\"I'm here.\"\n\n\"Are you all right?\"\n\nI looked at the notebook.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nThere was a long pause.\n\n\"Do you want me to wait?\"\n\n\"Yes. Outside.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nI heard him move away from the doorway. Not far, but far enough to give me space.\n\nThat was harder than rescue. I knew it was harder for him, because it was hard for me too.\n\nI stayed in the room for another twenty minutes. I opened the desk drawer and found a broken pen, two bus tickets, and a photograph turned facedown. I did not turn it over. I wasn't ready to look at a face I might not recognize.\n\nInstead I wrote the date in the notebook.\n\nI live somewhere else now.\n\nThe words looked absurdly small.\n\nI added: That does not mean this room has stopped being mine.\n\nWhen I finally stepped into the hall, Maui was sitting on the floor with his back against the wall. He had taken the opportunity to carve a tiny fish into the handle of his walking stick.\n\nThe wooden fish was magnificent only by Maui's standards: one eye sat higher than the other, and its tail looked like a badly folded map.\n\n\"I was going to turn into a gull and perform a dramatic patrol of the roof,\" he admitted. \"Then I remembered you said you wanted time alone. Very inconsiderate of you, frankly. Do you know how entertaining a gull patrol would have been?\"\n\n\"Was that hard?\"\n\n\"Unbelievably. I have a whole hook and nothing to do with it.\"\n\nMini Maui popped onto his shoulder and gave him a congratulatory pat, then fell over laughing at the fish carving.\n\nMaui glared at his own skin. \"You try carving without a proper knife.\"\n\nHe wanted to ask me everything about the room. The questions crowded his face. Instead he held up the lopsided fish.\n\n\"Present for the heroic survivor of a very boring afternoon.\"\n\nI took it. He looked so pleased that I nearly forgot how frightened I'd been behind the door. Nearly. The fear was still mine, and he was waiting to see what I would share. He'd earned that place beside me by not turning a private crossing into a show.\n\n\"You waited,\" I said.\n\n\"You asked.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd get bored.\"\n\n\"I did.\"\n\nHe held up the fish.\n\n\"I gave boredom a face.\"\n\nIt was a much better fish than the one on the broom handle.\n\nI laughed.\n\nThen I cried.\n\nThe tears surprised both of us. Maui stood, but he didn't reach for me until I stepped into his arms. His coat smelled of sea air and the smoke from Mara's bakery. I pressed my face against his shoulder and let the feelings come without arranging them into a story.\n\nAfter a while he asked, \"Do you want to go back in?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you want to close the door?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI turned, took the handle, and pulled it shut.\n\nThe latch clicked. Nothing in the arch dimmed. No bell sounded in protest. The Threadway did not punish me for leaving a room I wasn't ready to inhabit.\n\nI stood there for a moment, breathing.\n\n\"Is this running away?\" I asked.\n\nMaui considered the door.\n\n\"I don't think so.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you know where it is. And you're deciding when to return.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"That was a surprisingly good answer.\"\n\nHe grinned faintly.\n\n\"I'll try not to make a habit of it.\"\n\nWe walked back toward Tideglass in silence. Lanternwell Square smelled of bread and damp leaves. The frost was melting from the roofs. I noticed that the lantern I'd painted was still hanging beside the signal post, its wick extinguished and the circle of amber paint intact.\n\nBefore we reached home, I turned back toward the lantern by the signal post. The glass was misted from the cold. Somebody had replaced the wick while I was away, and the flame inside was steadier than the one I'd left.\n\n\"Who fixed it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Mara mentioned a volunteer on the morning shift,\" Maui said. \"They wrote it in the little service book.\"\n\nI found the book beneath the sheltered ledge. My earlier repair was still recorded there, along with the name of the person who had checked it today. The place didn't stop working when I stepped through a door.\n\n\"That's a strange thing to be relieved about,\" I said.\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"I suppose I thought everybody would be waiting for me to tell them what happened.\"\n\nMaui looked genuinely puzzled. \"We wanted you safe. That's not the same as wanting a report.\"\n\nThe words struck harder than I expected. I had a green notebook filled with memories of being useful, and somewhere in it a list I'd been afraid to show anyone. I didn't want those old obligations to walk out of the room with me and start issuing new orders.\n\nAt the house, I set my boots by the stove and looked at the fish carving Maui had left there. Its tail was uneven. He'd spent the afternoon sanding it rather than trying to follow me through the doorway.\n\n\"Can we have dinner before we talk?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And if I only tell you half of it?\"\n\n\"Then I'll know half.\"\n\n\"You're becoming dangerously sensible.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"Don't tell anyone.\"\n\nHe set out bread and warmed soup while I changed into dry clothes. Nothing about the food was magnificent, but I found myself hungry for the first time all day. When I returned, he had pulled out the chair I liked without making a ceremony of it.\n\nI took his hand briefly before we ate.\n\nI didn't owe him every word in the green notebook to be worthy of his company. I could choose what to share and still choose to stay.\n\nAt home, I put the green notebook on the kitchen table.\n\nMaui set down the fish carving beside it.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what you found?\" he asked.\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"A list I wrote before I came here. Things I was afraid to ask for.\"\n\nHe sat opposite me.\n\n\"What did you ask for?\"\n\n\"Mostly nothing.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\n\"That's not very you.\"\n\n\"It was me. Maybe it still is, sometimes.\"\n\nHe thought about that.\n\n\"I've been trying to learn how to ask instead of deciding for people,\" he said. \"Maybe you're learning the same thing from the other side.\"\n\nI looked at the notebook.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nI turned the page and wrote a new list. Not demands. Wishes.\n\nI want to visit the old room again when I choose.\n\nI want Maui to stay tonight.\n\nI want room for other people I love without making anyone a substitute.\n\nI want to know what the bell means, but not at the cost of my right to say no.\n\nI read the list aloud.\n\nMaui listened.\n\nWhen I finished, he said, \"I can say yes to one of those right now.\"\n\n\"Which?\"\n\n\"Staying.\"\n\nI felt heat rise in my face.\n\n\"You don't have to be so pleased with yourself.\"\n\n\"Impossible.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand across the table.\n\n\"Do you want company because you're sad,\" he asked, \"or because you want me?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Both. Is that allowed?\"\n\nHe smiled, but there was no triumph in it.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI went around the table and sat beside him. He kissed me softly, then paused when I caught his collar and drew him closer. The room was warm from the little fire. Outside, the sea moved in steady breaths along the stones.\n\nWe talked for another hour, about the old notebook and the strange relief of leaving something unfinished. We laughed about his carved fish, which had a suspicious resemblance to Fen.\n\nWhen I told him I wanted to go upstairs, he asked once more whether I was sure.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nWe left the notebook on the table and closed the bedroom door. The rest of the night belonged to us, with no demand to make grief beautiful.\n\nIn the morning, the notebook was still there beside the little wooden fish.\n\nI opened to the page I'd written.\n\nUnder the list, Maui had added a sentence in enormous handwriting.\n\nYOU CAN ASK ME AGAIN TOMORROW.\n\nI laughed so hard I nearly spilled my tea.\n\nThen I wrote beneath it, in much smaller letters:\n\nI probably will.","word_count":1690},"maui-19":{"number":19,"title":"The Apprenticeship of Asking","content":"The boatbuilder was sixteen, furious, and holding a plank upside down.\n\nI knew the plank was upside down because Fen had marked the side facing the water in bright blue chalk. The apprentice knew it too. They had been staring at the mark for nearly a minute, as though determination might turn the wood.\n\nMaui reached for the plank.\n\n\"Here,\" he said. \"I'll show you.\"\n\nLio pulled it away.\n\n\"I can do it.\"\n\n\"I know. But it'll be faster if—\"\n\n\"I said I can do it.\"\n\nI was carrying a bucket of clean water and trying very hard not to interfere.\n\nA moment later I interfered.\n\n\"Turn it clockwise,\" I said.\n\nLio turned toward me.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nI closed my mouth.\n\nFen watched the three of us with the expression of someone considering whether the dock needed apprentices, heroes, or a large sign prohibiting both.\n\n\"Start again,\" Fen said.\n\nLio put the plank on the sawhorses.\n\nMaui stepped back. So did I.\n\nThe new boat was a shallow coastal skiff intended for short journeys between the Tideglass landing and the sheltered inlet. It wasn't a legendary vessel. It wouldn't make anyone famous. It would carry crates of tea, rope, and occasionally the people who had jobs too small for a great voyage but too important to miss.\n\nLio had been apprenticed to Fen for six weeks. They knew how to measure the ribs, cut a clean mortise, and keep sharp tools out of other people's reach. They also knew, in the way teenagers sometimes know things very loudly, that they had to prove everything without assistance.\n\nMaui had volunteered to teach the knotwork. He had not been asked to build the boat.\n\nHe was having trouble understanding the distinction.\n\nBy midday Lio had finished fitting one rib and rejected four pieces of advice. Maui had demonstrated the same knot eight times. I had corrected two measurements that hadn't needed correcting.\n\nMaui knelt beside the practice frame and demonstrated the knot again. His hands moved with astonishing speed. The rope became a neat loop before Lio had managed to locate the working end.\n\n\"See? Beautiful. Effortless. You may applaud.\"\n\nLio folded their arms.\n\n\"Or,\" Fen said from behind us, \"you could teach a person instead of auditioning for your own statue.\"\n\nMini Maui appeared on his forearm and pointed at Lio's empty hands.\n\n\"You're all against me,\" Maui said.\n\n\"Only your teaching method,\" Fen replied.\n\nHe stared at the rope, thinking. Then he untied the knot and tossed the line back to Lio.\n\n\"All right, apprentice. Here's the trick: you tell me what you think the rope wants to do, and I'll tell you how badly you're lying to it.\"\n\n\"Ropes don't want things,\" Lio said.\n\n\"Then why do they spend their lives trying to escape us?\"\n\nLio laughed. They tried again, slowly. Maui kept his own hands to himself and narrated the mistakes with such extravagant descriptions that by the end of the hour the apprentice was calling every tangled loop a *demigod disaster*. Maui pretended to be offended, but he finally looked like a teacher who was having fun watching someone else learn.\n\nFen called a break.\n\n\"Both of you,\" they said, pointing to Maui and me, \"come with me.\"\n\nWe followed them to the workshop.\n\nThey shut the door.\n\n\"Maui, what are you teaching Lio?\"\n\n\"How to tie the rib lashings.\"\n\n\"No. You're showing them how quickly Maui can tie rib lashings.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\nFen looked at me.\n\n\"And you?\"\n\n\"I'm helping with measurements.\"\n\n\"You're taking the pencil every time the numbers look uncertain. What does Lio learn from that?\"\n\nI stared at my hands.\n\n\"Not much.\"\n\nFen nodded.\n\n\"This is an apprenticeship. They need to make mistakes within safe limits. We stop them before a mistake harms someone. We don't stop them because the work is taking longer than we like.\"\n\nMaui crossed his arms.\n\n\"What if the boat sinks?\"\n\n\"Then we don't launch it until it passes inspection.\"\n\nThat seemed obvious once someone said it.\n\nI thought about the last several months. Maui and I had spent so much time talking about respecting other people's choices that I'd become proud of how well I could explain it.\n\nExplaining wasn't the same as practicing.\n\n\"What should we do?\" I asked.\n\nFen handed me a blank inspection sheet.\n\n\"Ask Lio what help they want. Then give it.\"\n\nMaui looked at the paper.\n\n\"That's all?\"\n\n\"That's a surprising amount of work for people who enjoy talking.\"\n\nWe returned to the slipway.\n\nLio was sanding the rib we'd left on the sawhorses. Their jaw was set. They didn't look up when we approached.\n\nMaui stopped at a respectful distance.\n\n\"I think I've been showing off,\" he said.\n\nLio glanced up.\n\n\"You think?\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThey looked startled.\n\nHe held out the rope.\n\n\"Would you like me to demonstrate once and then let you try? Or would you rather work it out?\"\n\nLio considered the question.\n\n\"Demonstrate once. Slowly.\"\n\nMaui smiled.\n\n\"Slowly. I can do that.\"\n\nI tried not to laugh.\n\nHe took nearly a full minute to tie the knot. It was the least dramatic demonstration I'd ever seen from him. He named each loop, explained which part carried the load, and showed how the line could be loosened for inspection.\n\nThen he handed the rope over.\n\nLio got it wrong.\n\nHe waited.\n\nThey tried again.\n\nThis time it held.\n\n\"Good,\" Maui said.\n\nLio grinned before they could stop themself.\n\nI looked down at my inspection sheet.\n\nThe next step was mine. When Lio asked me to check a measurement, I didn't take the pencil. I asked them to read the numbers aloud. We compared the marked board with the drawing and found one discrepancy together.\n\nIt took longer.\n\nIt was better.\n\nAfter lunch we met with the person who had ordered the skiff. A market gardener named Sela needed a shallow boat for moving small tools during flood season. She wanted a low step, a stable handhold, and space for a crate that wouldn't slide when the deck got wet.\n\nLio had designed the boat with a lovely sweeping curve that would leave very little flat space.\n\nThey looked at the drawing, then at Sela.\n\n\"I could change the seat,\" they said.\n\n\"Would that weaken it?\" she asked.\n\n\"Not if I adjust the support.\"\n\nMaui opened his mouth.\n\nHe closed it again.\n\nLio looked at him.\n\n\"Would you check the lashing after I've made the change?\"\n\n\"Gladly.\"\n\nIt was a tiny moment, and it felt like something important had shifted.\n\nThe work continued for another week. We didn't suddenly become perfect mentors. Once, Maui started lifting a whole rib into place before Lio had asked for help. Once, I corrected a label that had merely been written differently from the way I preferred.\n\nBoth times Fen raised an eyebrow.\n\nBoth times we stopped.\n\nWhen the skiff was finally ready for the first water test, the whole cove gathered despite Lio's request for a small audience. The line of people along the shore looked like a festival crowd.\n\nLio stared at them.\n\n\"I don't want a speech,\" they muttered.\n\nMaui was holding a rolled piece of paper.\n\nHe looked down at it.\n\nThen at Lio.\n\n\"No speech,\" he agreed.\n\nHe put the paper away.\n\nI loved him a little more for that.\n\nThe boat went into the water carefully. Fen checked the trim. Tamsin measured how low it sat with the first crates aboard. Sela stepped onto the deck and tested the handhold. It was not perfect. The second seat needed to move an inch, and the floor got slippery when a wave came over the bow.\n\nLio wrote both things down.\n\n\"Can I fix them before we call it finished?\" they asked.\n\nFen smiled.\n\n\"That's the point.\"\n\nWe ate bread on the shore afterward. Maui had sand in his hair and a smear of chalk along his wrist. He looked tired in a way that made me think he'd actually been working instead of performing.\n\n\"How was teaching?\" I asked.\n\n\"Harder than fighting monsters.\"\n\n\"Less applause.\"\n\n\"Far less.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"How was not helping?\"\n\n\"Terrible.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI leaned against his shoulder. The skiff rocked softly against its line, unfinished but real.\n\n\"When I first met you,\" I said, \"I thought you were the person who knew what to do.\"\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I think the best thing you know is how to learn.\"\n\nHe kissed my forehead.\n\n\"Don't spread that around. It'll ruin my reputation.\"\n\nI grinned.\n\nLio called from the slipway.\n\n\"Maui! I need help with the final knot!\"\n\nMaui stood, then paused.\n\n\"Do you want me to tie it?\" he called.\n\n\"No. I want you to watch.\"\n\nHis smile changed.\n\n\"Coming.\"\n\nI watched him go, carrying nothing but his patience.","word_count":1466},"maui-20":{"number":20,"title":"The Second Bell","content":"The second bell sounded beneath the sea just after the Lanternwell rang noon.\n\nI knew the first sound. Everyone did. It was the old brass bell above the spring, low and clear, used to mark meetings and emergency route changes. Its note traveled over the square and down the canal.\n\nThe second note came a heartbeat later.\n\nIt was deeper. Not louder, exactly. I felt it in my chest before I understood it was a sound.\n\nThe water in the cove shivered.\n\nMaui stopped halfway through lifting a crate.\n\n\"That,\" he said, \"wasn't me.\"\n\n\"I hadn't accused you.\"\n\n\"You were thinking it.\"\n\nI wasn't. Not until he said it.\n\nFen emerged from the storage shed carrying the tide chart. The line of their mouth had gone flat.\n\n\"Inside,\" they said.\n\n\"Why?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"Because the tide's turning and I want to hear the next sound without everyone shouting.\"\n\nWe followed them into the shelter beneath the old signal post. Neri arrived soon afterward, breathing harder than usual. Tamsin came with a measuring rod. Danse brought a small instrument for recording vibration. Severus, who had been examining the damaged ledger fragment at Moonroot, carried a copy of the old diagram.\n\nIt was the first time I'd seen those people gather without somebody needing medical help.\n\nI did not find it reassuring.\n\n\"Did the bell ring twice?\" Neri asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"The second sound came from below the landing.\"\n\nNeri closed their eyes.\n\nMaui looked at them.\n\n\"You knew this could happen.\"\n\n\"I knew it happened before.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"Years ago.\"\n\n\"How many?\"\n\nNeri looked away.\n\nI felt anger rise, but I forced myself to listen. We had already learned that shame could make people guard information long past the point where it helped anyone.\n\n\"Tell us what matters for safety first,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded, slowly.\n\n\"The old service route runs beneath the spring and follows the water toward Tideglass. It was used to drain the floodplain during severe storms. It has been sealed for generations.\"\n\n\"We know about the route,\" I said. \"We need to know whether it's moving water now.\"\n\nThey looked at the tide chart.\n\n\"If it is, the seal may be under pressure.\"\n\nDanse set his instrument on the workbench.\n\n\"The second bell could be resonance. Moving water can make a cavity sound if there's an opening with the right shape.\"\n\nSeverus studied the copied diagram.\n\n\"Or a change in pressure could be moving an old metal fitting. The documents mention a warning device.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms.\n\n\"Oh, come on,\" he said. \"One mysterious bell beneath the sea. That's the beginning of a great story. Perhaps a lost kingdom! An ancient beast! A treasure so glorious it requires a demigod with excellent hair!\"\n\nMini Maui acted out all three possibilities on his chest. For the ancient beast, the tiny figure used both arms to make an absurd set of jaws.\n\nSeverus looked deeply unimpressed. Danse looked as though he wanted to inventory the imaginary jaws.\n\n\"I'll get an actual measurement first,\" Fen said.\n\nMaui blinked. \"You people know how to ruin suspense.\"\n\n\"I don't mind suspense,\" I said. \"I mind getting washed out to sea by it.\"\n\nHis grin became crooked. The hook was already in his hand; he wanted the adventure so badly I could almost feel it tugging him toward the stone.\n\n\"Fine,\" he said. \"We solve the riddle the boring way. Then—when it's safe—you let me name the ridiculous thing.\"\n\n\"Absolutely not,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"See? No respect for greatness.\"\n\nHe stepped back from the lower passage without pretending he'd stopped wanting to open it. That was the difficult part of the choice.\n\n\"So there's a bell under the water.\"\n\n\"Possibly,\" Danse said.\n\n\"That's what I said.\"\n\n\"Possibly is a useful word,\" I told him.\n\nWe decided on measurements before theories. Fen recorded the height of the tide. Tamsin marked the waterline against the landing stones. Danse set up his instrument where the old diagram suggested a service opening might lie. Severus compared the copied ledger with the notes Neri had brought.\n\nNobody went below the surface.\n\nMaui was unhappy about that.\n\n\"There's a tunnel,\" he said. \"I can go see it.\"\n\n\"And if it's unstable?\" Tamsin asked.\n\n\"I can get out.\"\n\n\"And if your movement collapses the channel and sends water into Sela's garden?\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nIt was an uncomfortable victory for the last several months of learning to ask.\n\nWe waited through the next hour.\n\nThe sea did not ring again.\n\nBy late afternoon the tide had fallen enough to reveal a pattern of stones beyond the repaired mooring. They formed a shallow arc. I had walked past them dozens of times. I thought they were part of the old breakwater.\n\nFen crouched beside them.\n\n\"That's not a breakwater,\" they said.\n\n\"Then what is it?\"\n\n\"A lip for an overflow channel.\"\n\nTamsin measured the gap between the stones.\n\n\"Someone built this to let water out without washing the landing away.\"\n\n\"Did it work?\" I asked.\n\nNeri stood behind us.\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\nThe word was so small it almost vanished in the wind.\n\nI turned.\n\n\"What happened the other times?\"\n\nThey looked toward the village.\n\n\"During the Closed Bells, the keepers used the service route to manage the flooding. They also used the same doors and locks to control who could leave.\"\n\nMaui's hand moved toward his hook.\n\nI touched his wrist.\n\nNeri continued.\n\n\"The people who made the decision believed they were preventing deaths. Some people were frightened enough to agree. Others wanted to go home and were told to wait.\"\n\n\"Who sealed the passage?\" I asked.\n\nNeri looked at the wet stones.\n\n\"That is part of the history I haven't finished telling.\"\n\n\"Because you're ashamed?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because someone living might be hurt by it?\"\n\nThey nodded.\n\nI wanted to shake an answer loose. I wanted the satisfaction of making the old silence end with one brave question.\n\nInstead I thought of Lark leaving through the eastern arch, and of the old register's expected-return column.\n\n\"We can't force a confession,\" I said. \"But the structural danger isn't private.\"\n\nNeri met my eyes.\n\n\"No. It isn't.\"\n\nWe agreed to post a public notice about the possible pressure change and schedule an engineering inspection. The route near the old stones would close temporarily. The cove would remain accessible by the safe path around the upper ridge. Fen would check the tide twice a day until the next survey.\n\nThat night, people gathered around the notice board.\n\nSome were angry. Some were frightened. A few insisted the second bell was a spirit calling them to the sea. Others wanted Maui to tear open the stone immediately and settle the matter with strength.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI did not answer for him.\n\nHe stepped forward.\n\n\"I've fought things that wanted to kill me,\" he said. \"I've made bigger mistakes because I was too sure I knew what the danger was. We don't know what's under there yet. I'm not breaking a wall while people live behind it.\"\n\nThe square went quiet.\n\nI felt a strange pride that had nothing to do with his reputation.\n\nAfter the meeting, I found Neri at Moonroot Archive. The old ledger lay between two sheets of protective cloth.\n\n\"Why the seventh scratch?\" I asked.\n\nNeri hesitated.\n\n\"Six public paths and one service passage. The mark wasn't a door for travelers. It was a way for maintenance crews to know where the old water route crossed the foundation.\"\n\n\"And my key?\"\n\n\"Shared pattern. The teeth were cut for maintenance hatches, not for ownership of the vale.\"\n\nI touched the brass key at my throat.\n\nI wasn't a chosen keeper. I wasn't the inheritor of a magical empire. For a moment that disappointed the part of me that wanted my missing past to mean something grand.\n\nThen I felt relief.\n\n\"So I can choose what it means now,\" I said.\n\nNeri looked at me.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nOutside, the lanterns were being lit along the safe path. People had already begun arguing about the inspection costs. It was a reassuringly ordinary sound.\n\nMaui met me beside the fountain.\n\n\"Any answers?\"\n\n\"Some.\"\n\n\"Enough?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\nWe walked home past the cove. The tide was rising. Beneath it the stones waited, not a prophecy, not a reward, but a problem our neighbors would have to solve together.\n\nThe bell did not ring again that night.\n\nI didn't mistake the silence for safety.","word_count":1409}}};for(const series of lib.series){const row=edits[series.series_slug]||{};for(const c of series.chapters){const v=row[c.id];if(!v||c.chapter_number!==v.number)continue;c.title=v.title;c.content=v.content;c.word_count=v.word_count;}}})();
